S1 replacement run — aborted by the prespecified quiescence gate after block 5
==============================================================================

PROVENANCE, read this first
---------------------------
This is a TRANSCRIPT of the operator's terminal, pasted back into the repository
on 2026-07-27. It is NOT a program-written record and has NOT been through
validate_results.py.

There is no JSON for this run and there never was. The abort raises SystemExit,
which is a BaseException, so main()'s `except Exception` did not catch it and its
json.dump never ran; and had it run it would have written an empty list, because
S1's per-baseline records are only appended after every block completes, while
the abort happened inside the block loop. Five completed blocks — 20 cells,
roughly 90 000 queries — were lost with the process. The abort message told the
operator to "keep this partial file", and no such file existed. That defect is
fixed for future runs (a salvage path now writes standalone-S1-partial.json,
marked RAW and admissible only for feasibility statements); the fix cannot
recover this run.

This transcript is therefore the ONLY evidence that this run happened. It may be
cited for what was attempted and how it ended. It may NOT be used to attribute a
throughput or tail-latency cost to the protocol — see the frozen judgement below.

Environment: Milvus standalone v2.4.15, single node, Docker, freshly initialised
etcd (`docker compose down && rm -rf volumes && docker compose up -d`).
Code: commit 0c38ec7. Command: `--exp S1 --full --s1-blocks 8`.


WHY THE NUMBERS BELOW MUST NOT BECOME A RESULT
----------------------------------------------
1. The preregistered design is eight blocks, i.e. two complete Williams cycles.
   Five is one full cycle plus one orphan block, which breaks position balance.
2. Block 2's within-block sentinel movement is 2.94/1.85 = 1.59x, past the 1.5x
   excursion threshold frozen before the run. At that tier the protocol cost is
   not attributable even for the blocks that did complete.
3. The run ended because the environment would not return to the calibrated
   band, not because it finished.
4. Every figure here comes from a terminal, with no validator and no committed
   record behind it.
Any one of those is sufficient on its own. Do not report a partial B4/B1 median,
do not fall back to cycle 1 alone, and do not run a third S1.

Within-block sentinel movement (P95 before -> after), computed from the log:
  block 1  2.28 -> 2.99  = 1.31x
  block 2  1.85 -> 2.94  = 1.59x   <-- excursion, > 1.5x
  block 3  1.93 -> 2.32  = 1.20x
  block 4  2.60 -> 3.79  = 1.46x
  block 5  2.12 -> 2.48  = 1.17x

Between-block recovery, time to two consecutive in-band sentinel readings:
  block 1 -> 2   172 s
  block 2 -> 3   193 s
  block 3 -> 4   275 s
  block 4 -> 5   111 s
  block 5 -> 6   did not recover; 12 probes, 316 s, hit the 300 s cap

Note block 4's B3 cell reports n_q=4489, not 4500. Do not write that every cell
completed its full offered load; describe workload completion from the record
fields (queries_offered, n_queries, queries_dropped, inflight_at_window_end,
drain_complete), which for this run do not exist.

No claim is made here about *why* the environment drifted. Compaction, segment
GC and page-cache effects are all consistent with it and none was measured, so
the paper attributes it to nothing.


